    {
      "title": "Delhi Police detains 30-40 people with criminal records before CJP protest",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/delhi-police-detains-30-40-people-with-criminal-records-before-cjp-protest-126101000082_1.html",
      "description": "Delhi Police has detained people with criminal records across all 15 districts of the national capital as part of preventive measures ahead of the Cockroach Janta Party's (CJP) proposed protest at Jantar Mantar, police sources said.\nAccording to sources, around 30 to 40 people with criminal records have been picked up from all 15 districts as the police stepped up security arrangements to maintain law and order.\nPolice are also keeping a close watch on the movements of certain people who could mobilise people or influence them to participate in the proposed demonstration, the source said.\nSurveillance has been intensified outside the residences of such people as part of the security measures, added the source.\nSecurity arrangements have been tightened across Delhi in view of the proposed protest.",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-10T02:35:45"
    },
    {
      "title": "Railways cancels over 250 trains ahead of CJP protest in Delhi on Oct 10",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/railways-cancels-over-250-trains-ahead-of-cjp-protest-in-delhi-on-oct-10-126101000011_1.html",
      "description": "In an unprecedented move that railway experts say amounts to \"surrendering to protesters\", the Railway Board on Friday has cancelled over 250 trains connecting New Delhi and Hazrat Nizamuddin stations with destinations across the country ahead of a protest planned by the Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) on October 10, demanding the removal of Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar.\nThe cancellations cover passenger trains scheduled to depart from their originating stations after 2 pm on October 9, as well as those scheduled to arrive at New Delhi or Hazrat Nizamuddin railway stations between 4 am and 2 pm on October 10.\nTrains scheduled to depart from these two stations for their respective destinations, along with local trains, have also been cancelled, taking the total number of cancellations to over 250.\nThe move has drawn sharp criticism over the inconvenience caused to thousands of passengers. A section of railway experts, passenger bodies and recognised federations have criticis",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-10T02:14:40"
    }
  ]
